  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\n\nLight rain continues to spread eastwards into western \nWisconsin, but should begin to diminish as we get later into the\nafternoon. Rainfall amounts will be on the order of a tenth of \nan inch or less. Skies remain mostly cloudy into Friday which \nwill keep temperatures fairly steady, falling to around 60 \novernight & warming back into the upper 60s tomorrow afternoon. \n\nRain chances return this weekend as a weak disturbance moves \nthrough the Upper Midwest. Saturday currently looks like the \nwetter day, with scattered showers & a few thunderstorms \ndeveloping across much of Minnesota & western Wisconsin. Similar\nto today, showers will be more widespread across western \nMinnesota compared to eastern Minnesota & western Wisconsin. \nExpected rainfall amounts this weekend range from around half an\ninch across western Minnesota to only a tenth or less across \nwestern Wisconsin.\n\nA stronger shortwave & surface front approaches the region\nMonday. Southerly winds ahead of this system should allow \ntemperatures to moderate somewhat, with highs climbing back \ninto the upper 60s to around 70 in many areas. Increasing \nmoisture will also set the stage for a more widespread round of \nrain as a cold front moves through Monday night into Tuesday. \nThis currently looks like the best chance for a broad, soaking \nrain during the next several days, with current guidance \nsupporting a widespread 0.50-1\" of rain and locally higher \namounts possible. Severe weather still appears unlikely, but \nembedded thunderstorms could enhance rainfall rates in spots.\n\nCooler air filters back into the region behind the front \nTuesday into Wednesday, with more fall-like temperatures \nthrough the rest of the week with highs in the low to mid 60s & \nlows in the 40s. The pattern may remain active as another \ndisturbance develops along or north of the stalled frontal zone \nlate Wednesday into Thursday. Some spread in the guidance exists\non just how far north the precipitation for this system may \nextend into Minnesota, but at least the far southern portion of\nthe state appears positioned for another round of appreciable \nrainfall. Otherwise, high pressure building over the Upper \nMidwest should keep us cool & dry through next weekend.",
